# trafficmon.deps — apt packages this agent's tooling needs.
#
# packaging/build-deb.sh turns this list (one package per line, # comments
# ignored) into the package's Recommends: apt installs them by default, and a
# name a distribution lacks never blocks installing Agenti itself.
#
# Keep this list in step with the agent's binary allow-list. A tool that is
# allow-listed but not installed costs a wasted turn and real API budget every
# time the model reaches for it.

# --- capture -------------------------------------------------------------
tcpdump
tshark

# --- signature-based alerting -------------------------------------------
# Packaged for arm64, installs cleanly on a Pi.
suricata

# --- protocol analysis ---------------------------------------------------
# Zeek is the highest-value feed: it emits structured connection, DNS, HTTP and
# TLS logs, so the model reads metadata records rather than packet bytes. That
# is both far cheaper and the main reason payloads never need to enter context.
#
# STATUS on this box (checked 2026-09-20): Kali Rolling DOES list
# zeek 5.1.1-0kali3 for arm64, so `apt-cache policy zeek` shows a candidate and
# looks encouraging. It is not installable: the package pins
# libc6 (<< 2.38) and this box runs libc6 2.43, so apt reaches conflicting
# assignments and refuses. Check installability, not just availability:
#     apt-get -s install zeek
# Alternatives if it becomes worth the effort: the OpenSUSE Build Service repo
# for Debian/arm64, or building from source (a long compile on a Pi).
#
# The harness degrades to tshark-derived connection records when Zeek is
# absent, so this is an upgrade rather than a prerequisite — trafficmon's
# run_zeek() checks shutil.which() and simply returns no logs. Uncommenting the
# line below once the package is installable is the only change needed; nothing
# else has to be touched.
# zeek
